# =============================================================================
# Quickshell module registration
# =============================================================================
# Every singleton and shared component in the shell is named here. This is what
# lets shell.qml write `Settings.features.dock` and a file three directories
# deep write `import ".."` and get the same object.
#
# WHY A SINGLE ROOT qmldir
#   Quickshell registers the config root as an import path, so one qmldir here
#   covers the whole tree. The alternative -- a qmldir per directory -- means
#   every cross-directory reference needs its own import line, and Lucid's
#   experience of that is a 61 KB single file written to avoid the imports.
#
# ORDER DOES NOT MATTER
#   QML resolves these lazily. A singleton is constructed the first time
#   something reads it, not when it is listed.
#
# ADDING A FILE
#   Components:  <Name> 1.0 <path>.qml
#   Singletons:  singleton <Name> 1.0 <path>.qml   (file also needs
#                `pragma Singleton` on its first line)
#
# THE ONE RULE THAT WILL BITE YOU
#   Only shell.qml gets these names for free, because it sits in the same
#   directory as this file. EVERY other file in the tree must start with:
#
#       import qs
#
#   Miss it in a file that uses a component (Card, Icon) and the load fails
#   loudly with "X is not a type" -- easy. Miss it in a file that only uses a
#   SINGLETON (Theme, Settings) and there is no load-time error at all: the
#   shell starts, the component renders, and every reference is `undefined`,
#   surfacing as zero-width layouts and blank colours with only a
#   ReferenceError buried in the log. Check the log after adding any file.
# =============================================================================

# The module name. Declaring one means every file in the tree can reach these
# names with a single `import qs`, from ANY depth -- rather than counting
# directory levels with `import ".."` / `import "../.."`, which silently breaks
# the moment a file is moved one level deeper.
module qs

# --- Configuration and policy ------------------------------------------------
# Settings is the persisted user state; Appearance turns it into design tokens;
# Performance turns it into "may this expensive thing run right now".
singleton Settings      1.0 config/Settings.qml
singleton Appearance    1.0 config/Appearance.qml
singleton Performance   1.0 config/Performance.qml

# --- Services ----------------------------------------------------------------
# One authoritative instance per system domain. Nothing else in the shell may
# open its own subscription to NetworkManager, BlueZ, PipeWire or the Hyprland
# socket -- they come through here, or state gets duplicated.
singleton Theme         1.0 services/Theme.qml
singleton Hypr          1.0 services/Hypr.qml
singleton Audio         1.0 services/Audio.qml
singleton Media         1.0 services/Media.qml
singleton Network       1.0 services/Network.qml
singleton Bluetooth     1.0 services/Bluetooth.qml
singleton Brightness    1.0 services/Brightness.qml
singleton Cava          1.0 services/Cava.qml
singleton Time          1.0 services/Time.qml
singleton NightLight    1.0 services/NightLight.qml
singleton Notifications 1.0 services/Notifications.qml
singleton Shell         1.0 services/Shell.qml
singleton ThemeCatalogue 1.0 services/ThemeCatalogue.qml
singleton Wallpaper     1.0 services/Wallpaper.qml
singleton SysInfo       1.0 services/SysInfo.qml

# --- Design system -----------------------------------------------------------
# The shared vocabulary. Every surface in the shell is a Card, every switch is a
# Toggle, every slider is a Slider -- which is what stops the Control Center and
# Settings looking like two different products.
Icon            1.0 ui/Icon.qml
Card            1.0 ui/Card.qml
Button          1.0 ui/Button.qml
QuickTile       1.0 ui/QuickTile.qml
Toggle          1.0 ui/Toggle.qml
Slider          1.0 ui/Slider.qml
Panel           1.0 ui/Panel.qml

